%S A125587 1,4,68,5008,1603232,2224232640,13288620860672,339148010221571072,
%T A125587 36646054311185413881216
%N A125587 Call an n X n matrix robust if the top left i X i submatrix is invertible for all i = 1..n. Sequence gives number of n X n robust real {0,1}-matrices.
%C A125587 An upper bound is the total number of {0,1}-matrices, 2^(n^2).
%C A125587 Comment from _Michael Kleber_, Jan 05 2006: A lower bound is 2^(n^2-n), A053763. For given the principal n-1 X n-1 submatrix A, the 2n-2 further entries (excluding the bottom right corner) can be filled in arbitrarily and then there is always at least one choice for the last entry which makes the matrix invertible.
%C A125587 Comment from _N. J. A. Sloane_, Jan 06 2006: Let the matrix be [A b; c d], where A is n-1 X n-1, b is n-1 X 1, c is 1 X n-1, d is 0 or 1. The matrix is singular iff d = c A^(-1) b, which for given A, b, c has at most one solution d.
%C A125587 Suppose A = identity, as in A125586. Then if d=0 there are 3^(n-1) choices for b and c, while if d=1 there are (n-1)*3^(n-2) choices for b and c. This proves the formula in A125586.
%H A125587 Samuel Orellana Mateo, John Urschel, and Nicholas West, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.08998">LU Factorization of Discrete Random Matrices</a>, arXiv:2608.08998 [math.NA], 2026. See Theorem 1.4 and Tables 1-2.
%H A125587 Samuel Orellana Mateo, John Urschel, and Nicholas West, <a href="https://github.com/Samuel-O-M/LU-factorization-of-discrete-random-matrices">LU Factorization of Discrete Random Matrices - companion code and data</a>, GitHub repository, 2026.
%F A125587 a(n) = Theta(2^(n^2)) (Corollary 1.2 of Orellana Mateo, Urschel, and West, 2026). - _Samuel Orellana Mateo_, Nov 16 2026
